==English==

===Etymology===
The pronoun {{m|en|me}} expresses the [[dative]], thus literally “woe is to me”, or in more idiomatic English “on me”. Compare the identical {{cog|de|[[weh]] [[ist]] [[mir]]}} and {{cog|yi|וויי איז מיר}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌwəʊ ɪz ˈmiː/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌwoʊ ɪz ˈmi/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-woe is me.wav|a=US}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{lb|en|sometimes|humorous}} {{ng|Used to show that the speaker feels [[distress]] or [[misery]].}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|3|1|passage=Blasted with extasie. Oh, '''woe is me''',<br>T'haue seene what I haue seene: see what I see.|page=266|column=1}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|120|5||'''Woe is me''', that I soiourne in Mesech: that I dwell in the tents of Kedar.}}

